1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:06,360
So, uh, if you're a government IT official running these large systems, you've probably faced this exact scenario.

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:14,440
Oh, yeah. Right. Totally. You, um, you hire a vendor, and their contract promises something like, uh, a working system and its documentation,

3
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:16,480
and well, they get to work.

4
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:17,940
And it sounds fine at first.

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:28,120
It sounds great. But, you know, the second you try to change a feature, like a year later, you realize the systems have become completely fragmented, siloed, and, um, just incredibly costly to change.

6
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:29,720
Yeah. It happens all the time.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:37,680
Welcome to the Deep Dive. Today, our mission is to, um, really look at how to manage technology suppliers so you prevent your systems from becoming duplicated or,

8
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:39,740
you know, locked into one supplier.

9
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,620
Right. Because that phrase you mentioned, a working system and its documentation,

10
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:47,320
is, uh, it's actually a massive risk for your ministry.

11
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,780
Really? Why is that so dangerous?

12
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:53,640
Well, I mean, it doesn't specify what you actually receive.

13
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:59,120
And crucially, you know, it doesn't state who on your side actually decides if it's good enough.

14
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,980
Oh, so you're just leaving the definition of success up to the vendor.

15
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:07,640
Exactly. You're paying them to build it, but they're basically grading their own homework.

16
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:11,080
Yeah, that's a direct path to getting locked into one supplier.

17
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:16,160
It really is. So to fix this, we need to look at specification-driven development,

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,240
or, uh, SDD. Right, SDD. Yeah. And instead of just one vague deliverable,

19
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,460
SDD breaks a service down into 12 distinct documents.

20
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:30,340
Oh, wow. Yeah. And they're delivered in a very fixed sequence.

21
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:36,700
It starts with the law or the mandate. That's the very first document, and it is never edited by the supplier.

22
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:43,320
Got it. And it goes sequentially all the way down to the 12th deliverable, which is, you know, the working application itself.

23
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:47,040
But, I mean, isn't requiring 12 separate deliverables just, uh,

24
00:01:47,660 --> 00:01:49,620
overcomplicating the procurement process?

25
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:51,140
Like, won't that just slow everything down?

26
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:58,340
It sounds like a lot, but no, because each document has to be completely finished and accepted before the next one is even written.

27
00:01:58,620 --> 00:01:59,020
Oh, I see.

28
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,780
Yeah. It prevents you from being locked in.

29
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,260
If they fail early on, you just take what you have to a new vendor.

30
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:07,200
So you always own the exact specifications.

31
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:12,580
Precisely. Okay, so how exactly should these deliverables be, you know, written into the contract?

32
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:16,760
Well, you have to name three specific things for every single deliverable.

33
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:17,720
Okay, what's number one?

34
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:21,800
Number one is the form, like the actual blank instrument it's written on.

35
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:24,880
So you know exactly what it should look like when it arrives?

36
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:25,500
Right.

37
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:31,160
And number two is the person who accepts it, but they have to be named by their role.

38
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:35,340
And, uh, the critical rule here is the supplier never accepts its own work.

39
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:36,480
Makes total sense.

40
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,260
And number three is the check it must pass.

41
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:43,000
So like a checklist, a comparison, or an automated program.

42
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:46,360
Okay, so it's sort of like, um, building a house, right?

43
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:48,800
You wouldn't let the contractor approve their own blueprints.

44
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:49,720
Exactly.

45
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:55,720
But, uh, how do we protect the ministry once the software's actually being built, like during the actual coding?

46
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:59,800
For that, you need two really strict clauses in the contract.

47
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:01,060
Okay, two clauses?

48
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:06,200
Yeah. Clause one: The application is generated entirely from the accepted model.

49
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:07,820
Meaning what exactly?

50
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:12,520
Meaning, uh, an engine builds it, and absolutely nothing is edited by hand.

51
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:14,480
Wait, really? No hand coding at all?

52
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:21,400
None. Because if the developer manually patches something, the system and the documentation are now inconsistent.

53
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:25,840
Ah, which makes it, uh, rekeyed and costly to change later.

54
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:32,240
Exactly. And clause two: Every open question is delivered with an owner and a date.

55
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:34,180
An owner and a date.

56
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:39,680
Right. Because if a supplier encounters a problem and just, you know, guesses an answer to keep moving,

57
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:42,060
that is legally a fault in delivery.

58
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:42,740
Wow.

59
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:46,160
Okay, so let's look at how this plays out in practice.

60
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:50,240
We have this example of, uh, the fictional country of Progressa.

61
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:53,580
Yes, Progressa. And their Ministry of Education used this.

62
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:56,800
How did they handle the acceptances in their annex?

63
00:03:57,220 --> 00:03:59,920
They explicitly mapped out four key acceptances.

64
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,700
First, the register of what was asked.

65
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,240
And who accepted that?

66
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:06,980
That was accepted by the director of higher education.

67
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:08,420
Okay, pretty high level.

68
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:08,900
Yeah.

69
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:15,240
Then the second acceptance was the screens of each goal, and that required a three-person review.

70
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,100
Three people. Who were they? The owner,

71
00:04:18,740 --> 00:04:24,060
the builder, and the user. So the actual officer using it is right there in the room.

72
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:25,640
Oh, that's incredibly smart.

73
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:27,420
What about the technical side?

74
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,380
That's the third one, the files a program reads.

75
00:04:30,820 --> 00:04:33,240
That was approved by the head of the ICT unit.

76
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:35,000
Got it. And the fourth.

77
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,280
The fourth is the working application itself,

78
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:42,380
and it's only accepted when an officer finishes a real task on it.

79
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:44,840
Not just a demo, but a real task. Exactly.

80
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:45,620
So

81
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:56,140
to summarize, to avoid those siloed systems, you have to explicitly name all 12 documents as deliverables, detailing the form, the acceptor, and the check for each.

82
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:57,960
And enforce those two clauses.

83
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,740
Nothing generated is edited by hand, and every open question gets an owner and a date.

84
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:04,400
Right.

